1- Data Visualization means representing data using graphs, charts, and plots so that we can understand the data easily.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2- Why do we use Data Visualization?
    comparision-comparing who scored more among
    Trends-we see whethere the marks are decreasing or increasing
    Patterns-we notice some repeated behaviour such as day 1 50customers, day 2 100, day 3 150 so 50 are increasing day by day
    Relationships- Are python marks related to math marks?
    Distribution-how are these marks spread?
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3- Matplotlib and Seaborn
--->Matplotlib gives
 more control over graphs, while Seaborn provides simpler and more attractive statistical visualizations.(Graphical representation of data)
    Matplotlib → Gives more control over the graph.
                 Can create bar, line, pie, scatter, histogram charts.
                 Works well with NumPy and Pandas.
                 Good when you need full control over the graph.
                 Requires More code for styling.

    Seaborn → Used for statistical data visualization.
              Creates better-looking graphs by default.
              Works very well with Pandas DataFrames.
              Good for showing relationships, distributions, and patterns.
              Good when you want quick and attractive visualizations.
              Requires less code for many common graphs.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4----

    Bar Graph: It is used for Categorical comparison
    EX: Alice   → 85
        Bob     → 72
        Charlie → 90
        There are separate categories.(we compare them in graph)

    DataType: Categorical + Numerical
    EX: Student → Marks
    It is used for Comparison
    → Categorical / discrete comparison
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Pie Chart: It shows parts of a whole.
    Ex: Python  → 25%
        SQL     → 20%
        Maths   → 30%
        English → 25%

    DataType: Category + Proportion
    It is used for Parts of a whole
    → Categories + proportions
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Line: 

    DataType: Ordered/Time + Numerical
    Ex: Test Number → Average Marks
    It is used for Trend
    → Ordered or continuous trend
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Scatter Plot: It uses two numerical variables.
    Ex:Is there a relationship between Python and Maths marks?
        x-python,y axis-Maths

    DataType: Numerical + Numerical
    Ex: Python → Maths
    It is used for Relationship
    → Numerical vs numerical relationship
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Histogram: It shows frequency(How many times something occurs) of numerical data.
    Ex: Here we want to know , How are the Python marks distributed?
        60–70
        70–80
        80–90
        90–100(ranges)

    DataType: One Numerical Variable
    It is used for Distribution
    → Continuous numerical distribution
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

5- Statistical data types
DATA
│
├── Qualitative--It describes categories rather than numerical measurements.
│   ├── Nominal--Categories with no natural order.
                    Examples:
                            Branch: CS, ECE, BBA, AIDS
                            (There is no "higher" or "lower" branch.)
│   └── Ordinal--Categories with a meaningful order.
                    Ex: Poor, Average, Good, Excellent(There is an order.)
│
└── Quantitative--Quantitative means numerical data.
    ├── Discrete--Discrete data is countable.
                    Ex: Number of students = 50
                        Number of cars = 20
                        Number of calls = 15(we dont say 15.5 students we say exactly 15 students so countable)
    └── Continuous--Continuous data is measurable and can take decimal values.
                    Ex: Height = 165.5 cm, Time = 5.72 seconds